# SQLite
*.db
# …and the migration backups the server writes beside it. `*.db` does not
# match a name ending in `.bak`, so a `git add -A` after running a server
# from the repo root swept 27 MB of real developer databases — users,
# credentials, chat history — into a commit.
*.db.bak
*.db.*.bak
crewship.db.pre-migrate-*
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# Pre-migration snapshots. internal/database/backup_pre_migrate.go writes
# "<db>.pre-migrate-v<from>-to-v<to>-<ts>.bak" next to the database, which on
# a dev clone is this directory. The suffix is .bak, not .db, so the rules
# above never covered it — one snapshot reached main at 7.5 MB before anyone
# noticed.
*.bak

# Go
/crewship
# `go build ./examples/pages/ping-go` with no -o writes the binary here, named
# after its package directory. One did, a `git add -A` swept it in, and 8.5 MB
# of ELF reached main carrying every Go stdlib CVE compiled into it — which is
# what the high-severity code-scanning alerts on /ping-go were.
/ping-go
/crewshipd
/crewship-sidecar
# `go build ./scripts/<tool>` with no -o drops the binary in the repo root
# named after its directory. That is how a 3.2 MB Mach-O arm64 executable
# reached main in #1480 — an enumerated list only covers the binaries someone
# remembered. Ignore every root-level build output for the tools under
# scripts/ instead.
#
# That last sentence was the right conclusion and it was never carried out:
# the list below stayed at two entries, and a third binary — 10.5 MB of
# docs-surface-check — reached main the same way in #2123. The list is now
# complete against every `package main` under cmd/ and scripts/, and
# scripts/no-root-binaries.sh fails the build if a compiled file is ever
# committed at the root again, because the next tool added here will not
# remember to update this list either.
/agents-invariants
/docker-api-surface
/docs-inventory
/docs-surface-check
/gen-openapi
/lint-migrations
/lint-tsformat
/memory-retrieval-bench
*.exe
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

